s), who in turn offered labor and loyalty.Calculated Indebtedness: Social currency relies on the principle that a gift or favor is never purely altruistic; it creates an unwritten, binding obligation for a future counter-favor (pridhaan or return-obligation).Accumulating Leverage: Men enhance their social standing (izzat or prestige) by acting as fixers, protectors, or benefactors, ensuring that a wide circle of people remains materially or socially indebted to them.", "author": "Stallion", "created": 1786645760843, "profile_picture": "", "community": "TheDonald", "ad_tier": 2, "last_comment_author": "PajeetaPunter49", "is_video": false, "video_link": "", "is_new_user": false, "domain": "", "is_crosspost": false, "preview": "", "removal_source": "", "link": "", "author_flair_class": "", "is_removed": false, "media": {"resized_resolutions": [], "blurhash": "", "full_resolution": []}, "title": "Indians can never become true Americans. Their entire social structure is the antithesis of traditional American generosity and upstanding citizenship.", "last_comment_created": 1786665988272, "content": "<p>Core Concepts of the Reciprocal/Favor StructurePatron-Client Ties: Historically rooted in rural exchanges where landowners (Jajmans) provided protection, resources, and security to service providers (Kamins), who in turn offered labor and loyalty.Calculated Indebtedness: Social currency relies on the principle that a gift or favor is never purely altruistic; it creates an unwritten, binding obligation for a future counter-favor (pridhaan or return-obligation).Accumulating Leverage: Men enhance their social standing (izzat or prestige) by acting as fixers, protectors, or benefactors, ensuring that a wide circle of people remains materially or socially indebted to them.</p>\n", "score_down": 0, "author_flair_text": "", "pro_tier": 0, "is_stickied": false, "is_nsfw": false, "is_deleted": false, "is_image": false, "comments": 9, "score_up": 91, "is_edited": false, "is_moderator